<!--
  MCU Ethernet receive buffer-pool config: the SSOT for the slots an
  Ethernet MAC's receive DMA writes frames into (ARCHITECTURE section 9.2:
  "the bus master writes the slot and the handler borrows it"), which lwIP
  then reads in place and, for a session datagram, the session dispatches
  from where it lies.

  Born with its consumer (the reassembly_pool pattern): `wz-link-lwip`'s
  `mac_rx_pool` module, behind that crate's `mac-rx-pool` feature, binds the
  generated receive lifecycle to a descriptor-ring MAC
  (`wz_runtime_core::MacRxPool`). Each receive descriptor of the MAC's ring
  holds one slot: the slot is armed for receive when its address is written
  into the descriptor, started when the descriptor is released to the
  controller, complete when the controller reports the frame written, shared
  read-only while lwIP (and a session socket that holds the frame) reads it,
  and returned when the last reader lets go. The descriptor is re-armed with
  another free slot at once, so a frame the stack holds does not hold the
  ring.

  Values (and their derivations):
    - slot_size 1536: the CYT4BF MAC's receive buffer length
      (DMA_CONFIG.RX_BUF_SIZE 24 units of 64 bytes), which holds a whole
      frame (1514 bytes without the FCS, 1518 with a VLAN tag) in one
      buffer, so a frame is never split across slots. A multiple of the
      alignment below.
    - slot_count 16: the ring's 8 descriptors (the T2G image's RX_SLOTS)
      each hold a slot, and the lwIP shim lets the stack hold at most 8
      received frames lent at once (WZ_ETHIF_RX_HELD_MAX in
      lwip-sys/shim.c); with 8 more slots the ring stays armed while the
      stack holds as many frames as the shim allows. A pool that runs dry
      anyway leaves a consumed descriptor unarmed, which the controller
      reads as no buffer available (a counted refusal, never a silent
      drop or a heap fallback), and the descriptor is re-armed when a slot
      comes back. 16 x 1536 = 24 KiB.

  cache-policy is `non-cacheable`, a statement about where the firmware puts
  the pool: the controller writes a slot behind the CPU's back, so the pool
  is placed in memory the MPU marks non-cacheable (on the Zephyr T2G image,
  Zephyr's `.nocache` section, beside the MAC's descriptor rings), and no
  cache maintenance is emitted or needed. The MAC refuses a pool that does
  not lie inside the receive window its board names. That section is NOLOAD
  and the generated Rust pool owns its storage inline, so
  `mac_rx_pool::install` writes the whole pool before it is used (a pool of
  zeroes is a pool of free slots).
-->
<scxml xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/07/scxml"
       xmlns:sce="http://sce.dev/ext"
       sce:kind="buffer-pool" name="eth_rx_pool_mcu" version="1.0">
  <sce:slot-count>16</sce:slot-count>
  <sce:slot-size>1536</sce:slot-size>
  <sce:section>sram1</sce:section>
  <sce:alignment>32</sce:alignment>
  <sce:cache-policy>non-cacheable</sce:cache-policy>
</scxml>
